-- title:
--   Transport of η-sections under an odd isogeny translate
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\iota : \mathbb{Z}_{p^2} \to W(k)$ a ring homomorphism, where `Zp2 p` is $W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ and `WittVector p k` is $W(k)$; $\bar\iota$ denotes the composite of $\iota$ with the quotient map $W(k) \to W(k)/pW(k)$, `pIdeal p B` being the ideal generated by $p$.
--
--   **The fixed special formal module and its rigidification.** $\Phi$ is a `FormalODModule p (W(k)/p)`: a commutative two-variable formal group law $F$ together with an action `act` of $\mathbb{Z}_{p^2}$ by formal endomorphisms and an endomorphism `varpi` with $\mathrm{varpi}\circ\mathrm{varpi} = [p]$ and $\mathrm{varpi}\circ\mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a)\circ \mathrm{varpi}$. The hypothesis `hΦ` says that $\Phi$ is special relative to $\bar\iota$ (the two Lie pieces are complementary and each is an invertible module), and `hΦ4` that $\Phi$ has height $4$, i.e. the kernel algebra of $\mathrm{act}(p)$ is finite and projective with fibre rank $p^4$ over every field-valued point. The hypothesis `hcΦ` asserts that the two graded pieces `Φ.gradedPiece ῑ 0` and `Φ.gradedPiece ῑ 1` of the Cartier module of $F$ — the elements on which the Teichmüller action of $c \in \mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ is the homothety by $\bar\iota(\tau(c))^{p^n}$, $n = 0, 1$ — are complementary; this produces the graded Cartier module datum $D_\Phi := \Phi.\mathrm{toGradedCartierModuleData}\,\bar\iota\,h_{c\Phi}$. The rigidification is an additive map $r_\Phi : \mathbb{Z}_p^2 \to D_\Phi.\mathrm{NMod}$, subject to `hrΦ`: for every additive $L : D_\Phi.M \to D_\Phi.\mathrm{NMod}$ that is a canonical $L$-map, $r_\Phi$ maps all of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ $\eta$-piece `D_Φ.etaPiece L _ 0`, the intersection of the $\eta$-subgroup attached to $L$ with the degree-$0$ piece of the $N$-module.
--
--   **The base ring and the matrix embedding.** $B$ is a Noetherian commutative $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, $\psi : W(k) \to B$ a ring homomorphism, and `hB` states that $p$ is nilpotent in $B$. Further data are a ring homomorphism $E$ from the centraliser in the endomorphisms of $F$ of the set $\{\Phi.\mathrm{actEnd}(a)\} \cup \{\Phi.\mathrm{varpiEnd}\}$ into $M_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$, and a natural number $m$, with: `hEinj`, $E$ is injective; `hEord`, for every $e$ in that centraliser there is $A \in M_2(\mathbb{Z}_p)$ with $p^m E(e) = A$ in $M_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$; and `hEcompat`, which requires that whenever $p^m E(e) = A$ with $A$ integral and $N_e$ is an additive endomorphism of $D_\Phi.\mathrm{NMod}$ which on classes `nMk (x, y)` is given by applying the endomorphism of the Cartier module induced by $e$ to both coordinates, then $p^m \cdot N_e(r_\Phi(w)) = r_\Phi(A w)$ for all $w \in \mathbb{Z}_p^2$.
--
--   **The isogeny.** $e$ is an element of the above centraliser and $m'$ a natural number; `hker` says that the power series of $e$ has kernel of degree $p^{2m'}$ (finite projective kernel algebra of constant fibre rank $p^{2m'}$), so $e$ is an isogeny of height $2m'$. Finally $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ with $g = E(e)$ as a matrix (`hg`).
--
--   **The two rigidified triples.** $t = (X, n, \rho)$ is a `Rigidified p Φ B`, consisting of a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X$ over $B$, an integer $n$ and a series $\rho$ over $B/p$; `ht` says $t$ is admissible for $(\iota, \psi)$, i.e. $X$ is special for the structure map of $(\iota,\psi)$, has height $4$, and $\rho$ is an isogeny of height $4n$ from $\bar\Phi_\psi$ to $\bar X$. The second triple is $t' = (X, n', \rho')$ with the same $X$, a natural number $n'$ and a series $\rho'$ over $B/p$; `ht'` says that $t'$ is admissible for $(\iota, \psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{m'})$, where $\mathrm{Fr}$ is the Witt vector Frobenius of $W(k)$. The hypothesis `htr` says that $t'$ is an $e$-translate of $t$ with parameters $k = 0$ and $m'$ over $\psi$: the underlying formal modules agree and there is $c \in \mathbb{N}$ with $\mathrm{act}_{\bar X}(p^{c+n}) \circ \rho' \circ \mathrm{frobSeries}(m') = \mathrm{act}_{\bar X}(p^{c+n'}) \circ \rho \circ \bar e \circ \mathrm{frobSeries}(0)$, where $\bar e$ is the reduction of $e$ along $\psi$. The hypotheses `hOD` and `hOD'` say that $\rho$, respectively $\rho'$, is a homomorphism of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules from $\bar\Phi$ (formed with $\psi$, respectively with $\psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{m'}$) to $\bar X$.
--
--   **Parity and constants.** $j$ is a natural number with $m' = 2j+1$ (`hm'`), and $c_0, c_1 \in \mathbb{Q}_p^\times$ are units with $c_0 = p^{\,j+1}$ and $c_1 = p^{\,j}$.
--
--   **Localisation and gradings.** $f$ is an element of $B$ and `Rigidified.awayHom f` the localisation map $B \to B_f$. Six hypotheses fix gradings over $B_f$: `hc`, `hcb`, `hcΦf` assert, for the datum $(\iota, \psi)$, that the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces of the Cartier modules of $X_f$, of $\bar X_f$ and of $\bar\Phi_f$ are complementary, with respect to the structure homomorphisms `Rigidified.jS ι ψ (awayHom f)`, `jSbar` and `jPhiS`; and `hc'`, `hcb'`, `hcΦf'` assert the same three statements for the triple $t'$ and the datum $(\iota, \psi \circ \mathrm{Fr}^{m'})$. Write $D_f$ for the graded Cartier module datum of $X_f$ built from `hc` and $D'_f$ for the one built from `hc'`; they have the same underlying Cartier module and differ in the structure homomorphism and the grading used.
--
--   Recall that, for a triple $s$ with integer $n_s$, an index $i \in \{0,1\}$, an element $z$ of the $N$-module of the associated datum and $v \in \mathbb{Q}_p^2$, the predicate `IsEtaSection` asserts two things: first, $z$ lies in `etaPiece L _ i`, the intersection of the $\eta$-subgroup attached to $L$ with the degree-$i$ piece; second, the lattice relation holds between $p^i v$ and the image under the reduction map `etaRed` (to the $N$-module of $\bar X_f$) of the $i$-th iterate of `nVarpi` applied to $z$ — namely there exist $m_0, k_0 \in \mathbb{N}$ and $w \in \mathbb{Z}_p^2$ with $p^{m_0}\,(p^i v) = w$ in $\mathbb{Q}_p^2$ and $p^{k_0} \cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(w) = p^{k_0 + n_s + m_0} \cdot (\text{that image})$, where $\mathrm{rigidNum}$ is obtained from $r_\Phi$ by transport along the base change of $\Phi$ and along $\rho$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** There exists an additive map $I : D_f.\mathrm{NMod} \to D'_f.\mathrm{NMod}$ such that:
--
--   (i) $I$ is bijective;
--
--   (ii) $I$ is the identity on classes of pairs: $I(D_f.\mathrm{nMk}\,x) = D'_f.\mathrm{nMk}\,x$ for every pair $x$ of elements of the Cartier module of $X_f$;
--
--   (iii) for every additive $L : D_f.M \to D_f.\mathrm{NMod}$ that is a canonical $L$-map for $D_f$, the composite $I \circ L$ is a canonical $L$-map for $D'_f$, and with respect to that canonicity, for every $z \in D_f.\mathrm{NMod}$ and every $w \in \mathbb{Q}_p^2$ the two equivalences
--   $$z \text{ is an } \eta\text{-section of degree } 1 \text{ for } (t,\psi,L) \text{ presenting } w \iff I z \text{ is an } \eta\text{-section of degree } 0 \text{ for } (t',\psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{m'}, I\circ L) \text{ presenting } c_0\,g^{-1}w,$$
--   $$z \text{ is an } \eta\text{-section of degree } 0 \text{ for } (t,\psi,L) \text{ presenting } w \iff I z \text{ is an } \eta\text{-section of degree } 1 \text{ for } (t',\psi\circ\mathrm{Fr}^{m'}, I\circ L) \text{ presenting } c_1\,g^{-1}w$$
--   hold, where $c_i\,g^{-1}w$ means the vector obtained by applying the matrix of the product of the scalar element `scalarGL cᵢ` of $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ with $g^{-1}$ to $w$, and the $\eta$-section predicates are `IsEtaSection` as unfolded above, taken with the data $(\iota, h_{c\Phi}, r_\Phi)$, the relevant homomorphism of $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules (`hOD` or `hOD'`), the localisation `awayHom f`, and the corresponding three grading hypotheses.
--
--   This is the common analytic core of the $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$-equivariance of the Cartier quadruple attached to a special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation, in the case where the height parameter $m'$ of the translating isogeny is odd: it interchanges the two graded $\eta$-pieces and twists the presented $p$-adic vector by $p^{j+1}g^{-1}$, respectively $p^{j}g^{-1}$. It is used to derive both the lattice half (the relations $N'_0 = c_0 g^{-1}N_1$, $N'_1 = c_1 g^{-1}N_0$) and the tangent-space half (the comparison of stalk maps) of that equivariance for odd translates.
